  "answer_text": "The iconic scene where Del Boy falls through the bar is from the episode **\"Yuppy Love\"**! Here are all the details:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcfa Episode: *\"Yuppy Love\"* \u2014 Series 6, Episode 1\nIt aired on **8th January 1989** on BBC1, drawing a viewing figure of **13.9 million**.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udf78 The Scene\n\nIn a wine bar, Del is leaning against a folding section at the end of the bar when he spots an attractive young woman. A barman comes along and lifts up the section Del was leaning against to get through, but doesn't put it back down, leaving a gap \u2014 all behind Del's back without him noticing. Del then turns to Trigger and says *\"Play it nice and cool, son. Nice and cool, you know what I mean...\"* \u2014 and leans back, falling straight through the gap onto the floor.\n\nJust as funny was **Trigger's baffled reaction** to Del's sudden disappearance.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udfad Behind the Scenes\n\n- The scene was inspired by writer **John Sullivan** watching a real man do the exact same thing in a wine bar, except the man grabbed onto the fixed part of the bar so he didn't fall all the way over. Sullivan found it funny watching the man's body language as he tried to recover his cool.\n\n- Sullivan originally wanted a slip, stumble, and tree-like fall, but **David Jason** thought Del should go all the way over \u2014 starting to go sideways, then falling without looking in the direction of the fall, which Jason felt was the key to the scene. There was a hidden crash mat, but it was a hard shot because it was difficult not to look where he was falling.\n\n- A rehearsal script that went up for auction revealed that **Trigger wasn't originally supposed to be in the scene** \u2014 the story goes that Roger Lloyd-Pack happened to be there on the day, and John Sullivan decided he should be included after all.\n\n- Roger Lloyd-Pack later revealed that Jason never did the fall in rehearsal, so it was a genuine surprise when it happened on the day of filming.\n\n- Jason gets people asking him about that fall all the time, and some never like to talk about anything else, but he's happy to be remembered for something so iconic.\n\n---\n\nDel Boy's fall through the bar has become one of the **most celebrated sitcom scenes ever**.",
